.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-START-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-start-application \- run an application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap start-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Records that this application should be running, and returns
immediately. The Euclid manager picks that up within a few seconds:
it materialises the artifact from its bucket onto the host, starts it
with the runtime's interpreter, and from then on runs it between the
application's minimum and maximum instance counts, restarting it if it
dies.
.PP
An instance is considered started once it creates the socket named in its
.I EUCLID_SOCKET
environment variable. One that does not manage that within the
application's
.I readyTimeoutMs
is killed and tried again, so an application that is merely slow to start
looks exactly like one that is broken - see
.B \-\-ready\-timeout
in
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1).
.PP
Nothing is reported here about whether it actually came up:
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-applications (1)
shows the instance count, and the application's own output goes to the
Euclid log.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The desired state was recorded. The definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, or no application of that name exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap start-application --application-id inbox
euclid-cli eap list-applications --prefix inbox
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-applications (1)
